WEBVTT

1
00:15:50.160 --> 00:15:52.560
I am an assistant
professor at Washington State University

2
00:15:52.560 --> 00:15:55.560
in the Department
of Digital Technology and Culture.

3
00:15:55.600 --> 00:15:57.840
And this is my project.

4
00:15:57.840 --> 00:16:00.240
A quick overview of this project.

5
00:16:00.240 --> 00:16:04.040
I'll talk a bit about the background
research on video game dialog texts.

6
00:16:04.240 --> 00:16:10.080
I'll mention a few details about this
fledgling corpus and where I intend

7
00:16:10.080 --> 00:16:13.440
to go from here, so you can access a link

8
00:16:13.480 --> 00:16:16.920
to the files that I put up on the site.

9
00:16:16.960 --> 00:16:19.840
But emphasize
this is very much a work in progress.

10
00:16:19.840 --> 00:16:22.840
My background is in linguistics
with a focus on French,

11
00:16:23.800 --> 00:16:26.120
and I've done some work on corpus
linguistics before,

12
00:16:26.120 --> 00:16:28.920
and I'm also a fan of video
games and RPGs.

13
00:16:28.920 --> 00:16:31.800
So this is bringing a few things together.

14
00:16:31.800 --> 00:16:34.200
We're looking at ludolinguistics here,

15
00:16:34.200 --> 00:16:39.240
and previous research has focused on 
recognizing video game dialog

16
00:16:39.280 --> 00:16:42.360
as legitimate sources
for discourse analysis, just like in film,

17
00:16:42.400 --> 00:16:43.920
books, other media.

18
00:16:43.920 --> 00:16:47.840
If we look at how language is created
and used in video games

19
00:16:48.520 --> 00:16:53.200
such as in RPGs,
previous studies have looked at how—

20
00:16:53.480 --> 00:16:58.560
male versus female characters,
there's a domination—

21
00:16:59.080 --> 00:17:03.600
dominated by male character speech,
about 70% in some studies,

22
00:17:03.600 --> 00:17:06.880
and also that male characters
tend to be associated

23
00:17:06.880 --> 00:17:10.200
with more objective speech,
whereas female characters are more

24
00:17:11.000 --> 00:17:14.320
receiving action that is done upon them.

25
00:17:15.000 --> 00:17:18.560
We can also use a corpus
linguistics of video

26
00:17:18.560 --> 00:17:23.800
game dialog to look at
how developers can create dialog

27
00:17:23.800 --> 00:17:27.560
that is gender neutral, or to use
non-binary language, not only because

28
00:17:27.560 --> 00:17:31.000
we have different genders of gamers,
but also as a way

29
00:17:31.000 --> 00:17:34.080
of creating dialog

30
00:17:34.080 --> 00:17:37.280
when we don't have the full context
from the developers.

31
00:17:37.280 --> 00:17:40.160
So for character, is writing to it
or speaking to another character,

32
00:17:40.160 --> 00:17:41.480
we don't know the gender.

33
00:17:42.960 --> 00:17:47.280
This could
pose a challenge for languages,

34
00:17:47.280 --> 00:17:50.280
depending on the grammar of the language,

35
00:17:50.320 --> 00:17:53.320
and we're looking at pre-scripted

36
00:17:54.160 --> 00:17:55.040
video game dialog.

37
00:17:55.040 --> 00:18:00.880
So we're not talking about voice chat
or gamer chats in like multiplayer games.

38
00:18:01.080 --> 00:18:05.880
Probably the best example of this corpus
approach is the Video Game Dialog Corpus

39
00:18:05.880 --> 00:18:10.280
that Rennick and Roberts and their team
at Cardiff University have developed.

40
00:18:10.280 --> 00:18:13.560
So they've taken several dozen RPGs

41
00:18:13.800 --> 00:18:17.520
and compiled the dialog into texts,

42
00:18:17.520 --> 00:18:22.280
into a corpus of over 6 million words
that's really available online.

43
00:18:22.280 --> 00:18:24.800
It's mostly in English.

44
00:18:24.800 --> 00:18:29.440
They have done some work with non-English
texts, such as in Mass Effect.

45
00:18:29.440 --> 00:18:33.280
They looked at how the English “you”
is rendered in French and Spanish,

46
00:18:33.280 --> 00:18:36.280
because you have to distinguish
between two forms of “you.”

47
00:18:36.520 --> 00:18:39.760
And they saw that localizations

48
00:18:39.960 --> 00:18:43.040
in this case
tend to be more overly formal.

49
00:18:43.040 --> 00:18:45.880
So this is a challenge for localizations.

50
00:18:46.920 --> 00:18:47.160
There are

51
00:18:47.160 --> 00:18:52.600
different ways that we can access
the game text to create these corpora.

52
00:18:52.760 --> 00:18:56.320
Sometimes it's as simple
as accessing fan transcriptions

53
00:18:56.320 --> 00:18:59.320
or community transcriptions online.

54
00:18:59.320 --> 00:19:01.760
It's just that these are heavily
dominated by English.

55
00:19:01.760 --> 00:19:05.080
There's very few non-English
game strips out there on the Internet.

56
00:19:06.120 --> 00:19:09.480
If you have access to the game files,
you can extract the text directly.

57
00:19:10.160 --> 00:19:13.560
It's just that this is not always easy
or technically feasible,

58
00:19:13.920 --> 00:19:15.000
depending on the game.

59
00:19:15.000 --> 00:19:19.240
And it is also, of course, possible
to just play through the game

60
00:19:19.240 --> 00:19:23.000
and write down the dialog, or increasingly
go on the YouTube and watch or play

61
00:19:23.000 --> 00:19:28.360
through or “Let's Play.” The problem,
though, is that these walkthroughs tend

62
00:19:28.400 --> 00:19:31.960
to not include
all of the possible dialog options,

63
00:19:33.080 --> 00:19:36.240
because in most RPGs
you have branching dialog options.

64
00:19:36.360 --> 00:19:38.080
And so we don't have a full

65
00:19:38.080 --> 00:19:41.640
we don't have the complete dialog
for a lot of these games, especially

66
00:19:42.920 --> 00:19:43.960
non-English games.

67
00:19:43.960 --> 00:19:46.600
And also access to
these videos can be removed at any time.

68
00:19:46.600 --> 00:19:50.440
So we don't necessarily
have a stable repository of these texts.

69
00:19:50.600 --> 00:19:54.480
So again, if you are looking for texts
in English, it's pretty easy to find them

70
00:19:54.480 --> 00:19:55.840
with a simple search.

71
00:19:55.840 --> 00:19:57.360
So there's several examples.

72
00:19:57.360 --> 00:20:02.680
If you take a game like 67, there's
the original or the remake versions.

73
00:20:02.960 --> 00:20:06.960
But if you look for other languages,
the text of this game and other languages

74
00:20:07.000 --> 00:20:09.960
in German, there is exactly one hit

75
00:20:09.960 --> 00:20:14.160
for a published script in German,
but it's just one playthrough.

76
00:20:14.160 --> 00:20:16.640
It's not a complete dialog.
Similar thing in Spanish.

77
00:20:16.640 --> 00:20:20.880
There's one publication online
that has the script as a playthrough,

78
00:20:20.880 --> 00:20:22.920
so we don't, again,
don't have the complete dialog.

79
00:20:22.920 --> 00:20:27.120
And then in French, there's pretty much
nothing at all in text formats

80
00:20:27.160 --> 00:20:32.000
available online 
for the dialog of Final Fantasy VII.

81
00:20:32.080 --> 00:20:32.960
So I did it.

82
00:20:32.960 --> 00:20:36.880
What I would consider it extensive search
of a lot of snippets of dialog, and I

83
00:20:37.400 --> 00:20:38.280
found nothing.

84
00:20:39.320 --> 00:20:42.760
So I thought that this corpus

85
00:20:42.760 --> 00:20:48.480
could give us a repository for these texts
that especially those that

86
00:20:48.480 --> 00:20:51.880
are in other languages besides English,

87
00:20:51.920 --> 00:20:55.240
and collect them in a single location.

88
00:20:55.720 --> 00:21:00.640
We can then, with multiple languages,
make cross-linguistic comparisons.

89
00:21:00.640 --> 00:21:04.560
So we would do parallel concordances,
where you can use an application

90
00:21:04.560 --> 00:21:08.840
like Sketch Engine to compare search
for how a specific word

91
00:21:08.840 --> 00:21:12.680
or phrase is translated
from one language to another.

92
00:21:12.840 --> 00:21:17.720
We can also add metadata so that we can
search for particular characteristics,

93
00:21:17.720 --> 00:21:21.920
such as the character who's
speaking or their age, gender, and so on.

94
00:21:22.440 --> 00:21:27.440
Okay, so I'll just give you a few details
with this fledgling corpus,

95
00:21:27.440 --> 00:21:31.600
and it's going to be based on Final
Fantasy VII, as you probably

96
00:21:32.600 --> 00:21:33.800
guess.

97
00:21:33.800 --> 00:21:36.800
And so right now I'm in the process
since I'm working with French.

98
00:21:36.800 --> 00:21:38.080
And I had to start from scratch.

99
00:21:38.080 --> 00:21:42.840
Basically, I have about 26,000 words
that I've transcribed

100
00:21:44.040 --> 00:21:45.960
from the English text that are provided,

101
00:21:45.960 --> 00:21:48.960
that have been provided online.

102
00:21:49.680 --> 00:21:54.400
I won't go into too much detail about
the process, but I did use the game text.

103
00:21:54.400 --> 00:21:56.360
I was able to extract the text from them.

104
00:21:56.360 --> 00:21:59.320
So this slide gives you those details.

105
00:21:59.320 --> 00:22:02.880
But I was working with a JSON text
that the Video Game Dialog Corpus

106
00:22:04.000 --> 00:22:07.600
has made available,
so I cross-reference with that.

107
00:22:07.640 --> 00:22:11.960
The JSON files
give you basically a readable

108
00:22:13.680 --> 00:22:15.840
text version of the files,

109
00:22:15.840 --> 00:22:20.640
and the Video Game
Dialog Corpus can be found on GitHub.

110
00:22:21.400 --> 00:22:22.320
I give you an example

111
00:22:22.320 --> 00:22:26.280
of the French version
that I've assembled with the link there.

112
00:22:26.280 --> 00:22:28.080
And that's on the Google site.

113
00:22:29.160 --> 00:22:30.440
The Video Game Dialog Corpus

114
00:22:30.440 --> 00:22:35.240
also has a visual representation
of branching dialog options.

115
00:22:35.240 --> 00:22:36.480
It's not just for Baldur's Gate.

116
00:22:36.480 --> 00:22:39.560
You can upload any JSON file
that's properly formatted

117
00:22:39.560 --> 00:22:42.880
and you get a visual representation here.

118
00:22:42.880 --> 00:22:45.880
So I hope to be able to incorporate
this in the future.

119
00:22:47.520 --> 00:22:48.800
So Sketch

120
00:22:48.800 --> 00:22:53.640
Engine is an online tool that allows
for parallel concordance extraction.

121
00:22:53.640 --> 00:22:57.840
What that means again
is, if we have an aligned corpus.

122
00:22:58.000 --> 00:23:02.320
So every line of dialog
in one language is aligned on the same row

123
00:23:02.320 --> 00:23:05.840
in an Excel file with every line of dialog
in another language.

124
00:23:05.880 --> 00:23:07.320
And we can use.

125
00:23:07.320 --> 00:23:12.240
Or I used our scripts to convert the JSON
files into the appropriate Excel format.

126
00:23:12.240 --> 00:23:13.600
So you can see an example of

127
00:23:13.600 --> 00:23:17.520
that, of an Excel file
that I uploaded to the Google site.

128
00:23:18.000 --> 00:23:21.240
You do have to sign— create an account
for Sketch Engine,

129
00:23:21.240 --> 00:23:23.880
so that would take too much time
to demonstrate here.

130
00:23:23.880 --> 00:23:26.360
So I just have some snippets
that I can show you.

131
00:23:26.360 --> 00:23:30.520
The word plates in English is important
in the context of this game,

132
00:23:30.520 --> 00:23:35.840
but it was translated
poorly in some examples of dialog.

133
00:23:36.840 --> 00:23:39.200
It should be “plaque,” the second example,

134
00:23:39.200 --> 00:23:44.720
versus “plateau.” So we can find
discrepancies much more easily

135
00:23:44.720 --> 00:23:50.600
than searching through individual texts 
using this parallel concordance option.

136
00:23:50.800 --> 00:23:51.600
Or we can go the other way.

137
00:23:51.600 --> 00:23:54.040
You can start with the other language,
like in French.

138
00:23:54.040 --> 00:23:57.480
If you want to look for the word “ami,”
or “friend,” we can see that

139
00:23:57.480 --> 00:24:02.040
there is some confusion
or possible ambiguity with the translators

140
00:24:02.040 --> 00:24:05.040
and distinguishing between
“friend” and “girlfriend” for example.

141
00:24:09.800 --> 00:24:13.120
And it's not just for comparing
two languages.

142
00:24:13.120 --> 00:24:15.160
We can compare multiple languages.

143
00:24:15.160 --> 00:24:20.120
I'm working on the Spanish
and German texts right now

144
00:24:20.360 --> 00:24:23.400
so that we can look at, for example,
an enigmatic expression

145
00:24:23.400 --> 00:24:26.400
like “to settle the score.”
How is this rendered across

146
00:24:27.400 --> 00:24:30.440
different localizations
and even fan translations,

147
00:24:30.440 --> 00:24:34.320
which is often the source of texts.

148
00:24:36.400 --> 00:24:37.120
And then an

149
00:24:37.120 --> 00:24:40.480
example with metadata we can tag
for a specific character, for example.

150
00:24:40.480 --> 00:24:46.560
So I give an example, screenshots
here from the Sketch Engine outputs.

151
00:24:46.760 --> 00:24:50.520
Which character references
the other character more often.

152
00:24:50.520 --> 00:24:51.920
So we see that.

153
00:24:51.920 --> 00:24:56.000
For example, Tifa mentions Cloud’s
name more often than Cloud mentions

154
00:24:56.240 --> 00:24:57.520
Tifa’s name in their dialog.

155
00:24:57.520 --> 00:25:00.520
So if the code for use metadata
to code for

156
00:25:00.600 --> 00:25:03.440
the names of characters,

157
00:25:03.440 --> 00:25:07.600
and the example of that Excel file
is also available on my site.

158
00:25:07.640 --> 00:25:10.640
Okay, so quick recap here.

159
00:25:11.840 --> 00:25:12.680
The main goal here

160
00:25:12.680 --> 00:25:16.360
is to create a central repository
for video game dialog,

161
00:25:16.360 --> 00:25:20.560
especially of non-English text,
because those are the most scarce online.

162
00:25:20.560 --> 00:25:23.120
And I would like this caucus to be useful

163
00:25:23.120 --> 00:25:26.560
for disciplines
connected to ludolinguistics.

164
00:25:26.560 --> 00:25:30.440
So as you can see, translation
or localization studies, discourse

165
00:25:30.440 --> 00:25:35.280
analysis, gender analysis,
and especially keeping with ELO’s mission,

166
00:25:35.360 --> 00:25:40.440
the preservation of these texts
and the free dissemination of these texts.

167
00:25:42.040 --> 00:25:43.560
Okay, think I'm out of time.

168
00:25:43.560 --> 00:25:49.080
I do have some slides on where
I would like to take this corpus, but

169
00:25:49.080 --> 00:25:54.320
I could answer some of those questions
in the Q&A, so I'll stop there.

170
00:25:54.480 --> 00:25:56.400
Thank you.
